\section{Parameterization}{
On every trial the reaction time is \code{t = mu + sigma * u}, with \code{u} a standard
Normal, and the choice is \code{dec = 1} whenever a latent decision variable
\code{w ~ Normal(mudec, 1)} is positive. \code{u} and \code{w} are bivariate Normal with
correlation \code{tanh(rho)}. The joint density of a response is then the
Gaussian density of \code{t} times the probit probability of the choice given \code{t}:

\deqn{f(t, 1) = \frac{1}{\sigma} \phi(u) \, \Phi\left(\frac{mudec + r u}{\sqrt{1 - r^2}}\right), \quad r = \tanh(rho)}

and \code{f(t, 0)} the same with the argument of \code{Phi} negated. Two things follow
from the construction, whatever \code{rho} is:
\itemize{
\item The \strong{marginal RT distribution is exactly} \code{Normal(mu, sigma)}. \code{mu} is
the mean RT and \code{sigma} its SD, as in \code{brms::gaussian()}.
\item The \strong{marginal choice probability is exactly} \code{pnorm(mudec)}. \code{mudec} is
the probit of \code{P(dec = 1)}, as in \code{brms::bernoulli("probit")}, so its
coefficients are the same quantities a probit regression reports.
}

\code{rho} changes neither marginal. What it moves is how the choice depends on
the time, which is the pattern the process models are built to explain:
the probit of \code{P(dec = 1 | t)} is linear in \code{t}, with a slope set by \code{rho}.
With \code{dec} coding errors, \code{rho > 0} gives \strong{slow errors} - the slower the
trial, the more likely an error - and \code{rho < 0} \strong{fast errors}. Recoding
\code{dec} the other way round flips the sign of both \code{mudec} and \code{rho} and leaves
the likelihood unchanged.
}

\section{\code{rho} is on the Fisher-z scale}{
\code{brms} has no link onto \verb{(-1, 1)}, so the correlation is estimated as its
Fisher-z transform: the correlation itself is \code{tanh(rho)}, and \code{rho} is free
on the whole real line behind an \code{identity} link. The two are close for
small values (\code{tanh(0.3) = 0.29}) and part company above that
(\code{tanh(0.5) = 0.46}, \code{tanh(1) = 0.76}); report \code{tanh()} of the estimate.

There is only one scale to think about: \code{rho} means the same thing whether
it is modelled in \code{bf()} or left out and estimated as a constant, which is
not true of any dpar behind a non-identity link. \code{rho = 0} is independence
either way.

The Stan code never forms \code{tanh(rho)} at all. Since \code{sqrt(1 - tanh(z)^2) = 1 / cosh(z)}, the argument of \code{Phi} above is \code{mudec * cosh(rho) + u * sinh(rho)}: no division, no square root, and no loss of precision as the
correlation approaches one.
}

\section{The same model as \code{gaussian()} + \code{bernoulli("probit")}}{
With \code{rho} fixed at zero this family \emph{is} the multivariate model below, with
the same likelihood, observation by observation:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# these two are the same model
brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, mudec ~ Condition, rho = 0,
         family = cogmod_gaussbit())
brms::bf(RT ~ Condition) +
  brms::bf(Error ~ Condition, family = brms::bernoulli("probit")) +
  brms::set_rescor(FALSE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The priors match too. \code{sigma} has the \code{log} link \code{brms::gaussian()} gives it,
and \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} leaves \code{mu} and \code{sigma} to the \code{brms} defaults, gives
\code{mudec} the \code{student_t(3, 0, 2.5)} intercept \code{brms::bernoulli()} would, and
leaves its slopes flat as \code{brms} does. The one prior it adds is on \code{rho}.

Freeing \code{rho} (\code{rho ~ 1}, or leaving it out of \code{bf()}) therefore adds exactly
one thing to the default analysis - the trial-level coupling of speed and
accuracy - and the difference in \code{elpd} between the two fits measures what
that coupling alone is worth, with the RT distribution held Gaussian. A
process model's gain over the \code{rho = 0} fit is what it adds over the
default analysis; its gain over the free-\code{rho} fit is what it adds beyond a
linear speed-accuracy dependence.

The multivariate form is worth knowing about, but it is not a substitute
once \code{rho} is free. \code{brms::bf(Error ~ Condition + RT, family = brms::bernoulli("probit"))} reaches the same likelihood by entering the RT
as a predictor, but \code{posterior_predict()} then simulates the choices from
the \emph{observed} RTs, so its posterior predictive checks are not joint, and its
choice coefficients become effects conditional on the RT rather than the
marginal ones above.
}

\section{What it does not do}{
\itemize{
\item \strong{No \code{ndt}.} The Gaussian's location is free, so a shift of the whole
distribution is \code{mu} itself: a non-decision time could not be told apart
from it at all, not even weakly.
\item \strong{No outlier component.} The Gaussian has support on the whole real line
and needs no floor, and the baseline is meant to be the plain default
analysis.
\item \strong{Negative reaction times have density}, as they do under
\code{brms::gaussian()}. The mass involved is small for ordinary RT data -
\code{pnorm(0, 0.6, 0.15)} is 3e-5 - and truncating at zero would cost a
normal tail per observation and break the exact correspondence above.
\item \strong{No \code{cens()}.} The choice is already modelled, through \code{dec()}.
}
}

\section{Fitting}{
\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{f <- brms::bf(RT | dec(Error) ~ Condition, mudec ~ Condition, rho ~ 1,
              family = cogmod_gaussbit())
brms::brm(f, data = df,
          prior    = cogmod_priors(f, df),
          stanvars = cogmod_stanvars(f))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{sigma} left out of \code{bf()} is a constant SD; \code{sigma ~ Condition} models it,
on the log scale.

\code{posterior_predict()} returns simulated reaction times and choices jointly, a
draws x 2 matrix as for the other choice families, and \code{posterior_epred()}
the expected reaction time, which is \code{mu}.
}

